  "response_text": "To allocate new stacks for use in multiple threads, a known, valid stack location in the memory is required to properly store said stacks. This can be done simply by referencing address of MSP_INIT_VAL in our code, since it is known to be a valid stack address. Since the number of stacks is known at compile time, it means that the program does not have to be given or calculated by the user, which saves the need to dynamically allocate the stack and compute stack addresses during runtime. A straightforward way to initialize multiple stacks now that the first stack created using the valid address of MSP_INIT_VAL is to create a reference pointing to the bottom of the existing stack, which should be repeated for each stack created as it grows downward. This can be done by subtracting the address of the top of MSP_INIT_VAL by the nth stack * the stack size. On the Cortex M4, since the stack memory space is predefined, it is important to determine if the number of stacks is valid to be created in said limited memory space. If the pointer is trying to reference an address at the bottom of the allocated memory address, it means that there is not enough space for all the stacks to fit.\\n",
